    "created_at": "2016-10-19T14:32:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-10-19T14:32:05Z",
    "body": "Oh boy I always learn new things. It appears that the simulated click code (called from calling click() on a HTMLElement puts the Node into a list of processing nodes; see https://cs.chromium.org/chromium/src/third_party/WebKit/Source/core/events/EventDispatcher.cpp?sq=package:chromium&rcl=1476847656&l=87.\n\nAnd if click() is called while that is on the stack it is avoided. This doesn't apply to dispatching click events directly via dispatchEvent; only calls that dispatch via the dispatchSimulatedClick.\n\nAs you pointed out there are a number of scenarios that do this. Look for callees of the dispatchSimulatedClick here:\nhttps://cs.chromium.org/chromium/src/third_party/WebKit/Source/core/dom/Node.h?q=Node::dispatchSimulatedClick&sq=package:chromium&l=735&dr=Ss\n\nAnd they are keyboard events (enter, space depends on context), access keys, label elements redispatch on click.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
